<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "https://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for the bundled Node runtime, and for nothing else.

  NOTE TO WHOEVER EDITS THIS COMMENT: an XML comment may not contain two
  hyphens in a row. Writing a codesign flag in here the natural way is enough
  to break it, and the way it breaks is nasty. AMFI reports "Failed to parse
  entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 24", codesign exits
  1, and the binary keeps whatever signature it already had. If nobody checks
  the exit status, the build carries on and ships a Node signed by the Node
  Foundation, with the Node Foundation's entitlements, and the sign step looks
  like it ran. That is exactly what happened here on 7 August 2026, and it is
  why package-app.sh now verifies the Team ID after signing this file rather
  than trusting that codesign did anything.

  To check this file, run xmllint over it (the exact command is in the failure
  message package-app.sh prints, where a comment cannot mangle it). Use xmllint
  and NOT plutil: plutil reports OK on a file with a double hyphen in a
  comment, so it cannot see the one fault most likely to be introduced here.
  xmllint says "Double hyphen within comment" and gives the line number.

  Written without naming the flag, because naming it is what broke this file
  the second time: the paragraph warning about double hyphens contained one.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. The Node binary from nodejs.org is already
  signed, hardened, and carries six entitlements of its own:

      com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
      com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection
      com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      com.apple.security.get-task-allow

  The last one is the reason this cannot simply be shipped as it arrives.
  get-task-allow lets another process attach to it as a debugger, it is a
  development entitlement, and Apple's notary service rejects it. A release
  that staged Node unchanged would fail notarisation at the very end of the
  build, after everything else had been signed and paid for.

  The rest are wider than this app needs. Node grants them so that any Node
  program can run: native addons that want library validation off, profilers
  that want executable pages writable. The bridge in whatsapp/ has no native
  addons at all, deliberately, and provision-whatsapp-bridge.sh fails if one
  ever appears.

  WHAT IS GRANTED, AND WHAT IT COST TO FIND OUT. V8 compiles JavaScript to
  machine code at runtime, so it needs permission to map executable memory.
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit is the narrow form of that permission: it
  allows MAP_JIT, which respects W^X, and it is what V8 uses on Apple Silicon.
  The wider com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory turns the
  protection off rather than carving a hole in it, and is not granted.

  The measurements that settled it are in the header of
  scripts/provision-node.sh, taken with the real Developer ID identity rather
  than an ad-hoc signature. That distinction matters: an ad-hoc signature has
  no Team ID, so the hardened runtime is not enforced and a development build
  will happily run a binary a signed release would kill. It is the same trap
  resources/SignalCLI.entitlements describes from the other direction.
-->
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
